= Meaning representation — Technical =

== Design dimensions ==
Meaning-representation formalisms differ along dimensions such as:

* expressivity;
* compositionality;
* underspecification;
* graph versus logical structure;
* ontology dependence;
* treatment of scope;
* event semantics;
* discourse accessibility;
* context dependence;
* inference regime;
* annotation cost.

Increasing expressivity can make annotation, parsing, and reasoning harder.

== Logical representation ==
A typed or first-order logical form can make truth-conditional structure explicit.

For example:

<pre>
∃e∃x(
  write(e)
  ∧ agent(e, Ada)
  ∧ theme(e, x)
  ∧ program(x)
)
</pre>

This exposes event and role structure but still omits many pragmatic and discourse commitments.

== Discourse representation ==
DRT-style formalisms use discourse representation structures containing discourse referents and conditions.

Accessibility constraints provide a mechanism for anaphora and quantified expressions across sentence boundaries.

== Graph formalisms ==
AMR and semantic dependency representations encode predicate–argument or conceptual structure as labeled graphs.

Graph normalization can abstract away from surface syntax, but annotation conventions define what distinctions survive.

== Parsing and evaluation ==
A semantic parser estimates:

<pre>
argmax_m P(m | utterance, context)
</pre>

for target representation m.

Exact-match or graph-overlap scores measure agreement with a target annotation scheme, not universal correctness of meaning.

== Symbolic and neural representations ==
Dense embeddings can condition or generate symbolic structures; symbolic structures can constrain neural inference.

Hybrid architecture does not erase the distinction between distributed numerical representation and explicit semantic structure.

